<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tyrol</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tyrol is a historical region in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, centred on the valleys of the Inn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adige">Adige</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisack">Eisack</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brenner-pass">Brenner Pass</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> land from 1363, it was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> by the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a> in 1805, provoking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andreas-hofer">Andreas Hofer</a>'s rising of 1809, and was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1814. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it was divided between Austria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tyrol.jpg" alt="Tyrol" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Nightstallion assumed (based on copyright claims). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tirol_and_Upper_Austria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
